**Goal:** read an [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) type and say which services the work still needs.

An Effect type has three parts: what the work returns, how it can fail, and which services it needs.
The third part is the requirements.
A context is the set of services that running work receives.

## Worked example: inspecting a file

rat-stack's file inspection reads a file through a `FileInspector` [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies).
The [actor](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines) that does the read asks for that service by name:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=27-31 {29} title="The read asks for FileInspector"

```

`FileInspector.use` returns an Effect whose requirements include `FileInspector`.
Nothing runs yet.
The type now records a debt: some caller must supply a `FileInspector`.

The CLI pays that debt once, at startup:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/cli/src/cli.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=22-24 {24} title="The composition root supplies it"

```

`Effect.provide` removes `FileInspector` from the requirements.
`FileInspector.layer` itself needs a file system, and `NodeServices.layer` supplies it.

The tests pay the same debt with their own Layer:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/test/inspect-machine.test.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=14 title="A test supplies the same requirement"

```

The diagram above shows the same move on a small `Greeter` service: `provide` turns `Greeter` into `never`.

## Why the type carries it

The compiler rejects execution while a required service remains unsupplied. [Dependency injection](/lore/dependency-injection-is-the-reason-to-choose-effect) shows that rule.
A forgotten service fails the typecheck before the program runs.

Sam Goodwin describes this requirements channel as a way to model permissions before execution, which he calls a static effect.
He connects that idea to [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy) inferring which identity and access management (IAM) policy a program needs. [Happy Path 116, 11:54](https://youtu.be/YEn4A7XxgF4?t=714)

Michael Arnaldi describes an Effect by its output, predictable failures, and requirements. [Happy Path 81, 49:15](https://youtu.be/gc5P36t6kD0?t=2955)

